---
<p class="lede">You build <code>bgrep</code>: it takes a pattern and files, prints the matching lines, and sets an exit code scripts can use. It uses structs, errors, effects, capabilities and tests, in steps, then a project.</p>
<pre><code>bgrep [-i] [-n] [-c] PATTERN FILE...

-i   ignore case
-n   print line numbers
-c   print only a count per file

exit 0: at least one line matched
exit 1: nothing matched
exit 2: usage error or unreadable file</code></pre>

<h2 id="args">Step 1: read the arguments</h2>
<p>A command-line program is a <code>main</code> taking its arguments as a <code>ReadOnlySpan&lt;string&gt;</code>; parsing is a plain function returning <code>Options</code> or failing. Bad input is a usage error with its own error set:</p>
<example name="book-18-args"/>
<ul>
<li>The error set is a <code>union error</code>; a case's doc comment is what <code>main</code> prints if that case reaches the top (step 4).</li>
<li><code>fail .unknownOption(arg)</code> carries the bad flag out; <code>fail .missingPattern unless havePattern</code> requires a pattern.</li>
<li><code>parseArgs</code> says <code>fails UsageError</code> (the caller must handle it) and <code>uses alloc</code> (it builds the file list).</li>
<li><code>main</code> passes built-in arguments, so the example needs none; <code>check</code> reads the cases with <code>catch switch</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="match">Step 2: decide if a line matches</h2>
<p>Matching belongs to <code>Options</code>, since only it knows about <code>-i</code>; a read-only method leaves <code>this</code> out of its parameter list:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">bool matches(string line) uses alloc {
    if ignoreCase {
        return line.lowercased().contains(pattern.lowercased())
    }
    return line.contains(pattern)
}</code></pre>
<p><code>lowercased()</code> makes new strings, so it says <code>uses alloc</code>.</p>

<h2 id="search">Step 3: search one file</h2>
<p><code>search</code> takes the text, the options, an output name and whether to prefix lines with it. Bayan allows one plain parameter, the object; the rest need a particle (<code>by</code>, <code>as</code>, <code>to</code>). Without one:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 search(string text, string name) {
    return text.length + name.length
}

void main() uses console {
    print($"{search("a", "b")}")
}</code></pre>
    <figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN5004:</span> `search` has 2 unlabeled parameters; give each but one a particle
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> two-params.byn:1:5

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> i64 <span class="hl">search</span>(string text, string name) {
<span class="gut">    │</span>     <span class="hl">^^^^^^ 2 unlabeled parameters</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> keep one unlabeled parameter, the object

<span class="err">error BYN5006:</span> `name` is a required parameter besides the object and needs a particle
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> two-params.byn:1:32

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> i64 search(string text, string <span class="hl">name</span>) {
<span class="gut">    │</span>                                <span class="hl">^^^^ needs a particle</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> label it with `from`, `to`, `until`, `through`, `at`, `by`, `for` or `as`

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 2 errors, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN5004</span></pre>
      <figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened to <code>two-params.byn</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
<p>The final program fixes it: <code>search(string text, by Options opts, as string name, to bool showName)</code>; a call reads <code>search(text, by: opts, as: path, to: true)</code>.</p>
<p>The whole tool in one file, runnable as is: with no arguments it writes a small file and searches it; the loop uses <code>text.lines</code>, and <code>Io.readText</code>'s <code>catch</code> reports the problem on standard error and moves to the next file.</p>
<example name="book-18-bgrep"/>
<p>The demo ran <code>-n -i APPLE</code>: <code>-i</code> matched <code>Green Apple</code>, <code>-n</code> numbered the line. The exit code is <code>main</code>'s return value: 0, two lines matched.</p>

<h2 id="project">Step 4: make it a project</h2>
<p>Create the project and split the code into modules; a directory is one module, and a <code>public</code> name is visible to the project's other files.</p>
<pre><code>bayan new bgrep
bgrep/
  package.byn
  src/app/main.byn       main
  src/app/options.byn    Options, parseArgs, UsageError
  src/app/search.byn     search
  tests/app.test.byn
  testdata/fruit.txt</code></pre>
<p>The search reads files, so the package must allow it (<a href="book-14-effects.html">chapter 14</a>); in <code>package.byn</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">capabilities: [.fs],</code></pre>
<p>Mark the types, functions and error set other files use <code>public</code>, and give each public function its <code>uses</code> clause. Run it with <code>bayan run</code>; arguments go after <code>--</code>. Real runs on two files in <code>testdata/</code>:</p>
<pre><code>$ bayan run -- apple testdata/fruit.txt
one apple
three apple pie
$ echo $?
0
$ bayan run -- zzz testdata/fruit.txt
$ echo $?
1
$ bayan run -- -c a testdata/fruit.txt testdata/more.txt
testdata/fruit.txt: 3
testdata/more.txt: 1
$ bayan run -- -x file
error: Unknown option.
$ echo $?
2</code></pre>
<p>The last run shows the doc comment from step 1: the error reached the top of <code>main</code>, so it printed the case's text and exited; <code>@exitCode(2)</code> above a case sets that status. With several files each line starts with the file name (the <code>-c</code> run).</p>

<h2 id="tests">Step 5: test it</h2>
<p>Tests live in <code>tests/</code> and can call the public functions; <code>assert f() fails Error.case</code> checks a call fails with the given error.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">test "parses flags, pattern and files" {
    let args: string[4] = ["-n", "apple", "a.txt", "b.txt"]
    let opts = try parseArgs(args.span)
    assert opts.numbers
    assert !opts.ignoreCase
    assert opts.pattern == "apple"
    assert opts.files.length == 2
}

test "a missing pattern is a usage error" {
    let none: string[1] = ["-i"]
    assert parseArgs(none.span) fails UsageError.missingPattern
}

test "search counts matching lines" {
    let args: string[2] = ["-c", "apple"]
    let opts = try parseArgs(args.span)
    assert search("one apple\ntwo\nthree apple\n", by: opts, as: "t.txt", to: false) == 2
}</code></pre>
<p><code>bayan test</code> on the project, five tests:</p>
<pre><code>running 5 tests in 1 module

◆ 5 passed</code></pre>

<h2 id="next">Where to go next</h2>
<p>Add a <code>-v</code> flag that inverts the match, or read standard input with no files. The <a href="cookbook-cli.html">CLI cookbook</a> covers arguments and exit codes.</p>

<h2 id="quiz">Check your understanding</h2>
<details class="quiz"><summary>1. The demo in <code>book-18-bgrep</code> prints <code>3:Green Apple</code>. Which flags matched and numbered the line?</summary>
<p><code>-i</code> let <code>APPLE</code> match <code>Green Apple</code>, <code>-n</code> added the line number; without <code>-i</code> no match, <code>-c</code> would print a count instead.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>2. What exit code does <code>bgrep zzz fruit.txt</code> return with no match?
<ol type="a"><li>0</li><li>1</li><li>2</li></ol></summary>
<p>b: 0 is a match, 2 is for usage errors and unreadable files; no match is not an error.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>3. Why does <code>search(string text, string name)</code> not compile?</summary>
<p>Two unlabeled parameters (BYN5004); keep the object plain and give <code>name</code> a particle such as <code>as</code> (BYN5006).</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>4. The package builds locally but BYN6004 appears when you add <code>Io.readText</code>. What is missing?</summary>
<p><code>capabilities: [.fs]</code> in <code>package.byn</code>: the functions say <code>uses fs</code>; the package has not allowed it.</p></details>

